## 6. Cost basis for tickets (HPP/COGS)

**None exists, and none is planned.** Confirmed by reading the full `ticket_categories`, `ticket_serials`, and `ticket_transactions` schemas — no `cost` column anywhere in any of them, and no `total_cost`/`gross_profit` columns on `ticket_transactions` (contrast with `public.transactions` which has `total_cost numeric(12,2)` and `gross_profit numeric(12,2)`, used to drive `v_total_cost` in `checkout_transaction`).

The migration's own top-of-file comment makes this an explicit design decision (`20260706200000_ticket_venue.sql`, lines 97–100):

> "Ticket transactions (header) ... Mirip public.transactions tapi untuk penjualan tiket — sengaja dipisah (bukan union ke transactions) karena **tidak ada konsep HPP/COGS untuk tiket masuk**, dan halaman laporan yang sudah ada tetap scoped ke transactions."

("Similar to public.transactions but for ticket sales — deliberately kept separate (not unioned into transactions) because **there's no HPP/COGS concept for entry tickets**, and existing report pages stay scoped to transactions.")

So your journal entry for a ticket sale should be **revenue-only, no HPP/Persediaan lines, ever** — there's no `v_total_cost`-equivalent variable to compute or gate on. The entry is simply: debit `1-001 Kas & Bank` for `v_total`, credit revenue account for `v_subtotal + v_service`, and conditionally credit `2-200 PPN Keluaran` for `v_tax` if tax is enabled — a 2-or-3-line entry, full stop, following the exact shape of the *first half* of `checkout_transaction`'s journal block (§3) but omitting the HPP/Persediaan `if v_total_cost > 0` block entirely since that condition can never be true for tickets.

